      "value": "@{5f2e0a4e8464150716ffd322|full_name} due to how unity's physics and by extent it's raycasts work, the length of a raycast doesn't actually have a performance impact inherently. \n\nthis may need some fact checking as it's been a while since i went deep into this but, If i remember correctly in a vacuum a 1m raycast and a 1000m raycast would perform the exact same. \n\nthe thing that makes a difference is how many colliders the ray would pass by without colliding with any. as when the ray casts through the bounding box of an object with a collider that is the moment where the collider is checked if it intersects with the ray. so the more objects you just barely miss, the more of an impact you have on performance. if you hit the first thing infront of you that's the end of it and at that point there's no difference between a 1m raycast or a 1000m raycast. \n\nof course this is also affected by layers and other contact rules. so there's some variance with it layer checking colliders and passing through them etc etc. but in most cases raycasts should be really fast either way.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
